  "historyText": "Zig Zag is a 1982 unlicensed reworking of Namco's Dig Dug, credited on screen\nto LAX. This set is not merely a renamed Dig Dug ROM: it was rewritten for a\nmodified Moon Cresta/Galaxian-style board with one Z80 and one AY-8910.\n\nThe rules changed with the hardware. The pump is gone, enemies are defeated at\nclose range, and a temporary pickaxe power-up speeds digging. Community\ncatalogs also record the missing vegetable bonuses and a round-99 overflow or\nkill screen. The result is recognisably inspired by Dig Dug but behaves like\nits own compact conversion.\n\n- HARDWARE AND COPY PROTECTION -\n\nMAME models two 4 KiB program windows. Zig Zag can exchange the second and\nthird program ROMs while running; the MAME driver explicitly describes this as\ncopy protection. It is bank switching, not encrypted Z80 opcodes.\n\nThe graphics packaging is unusual too. Each physical graphics ROM contains two\nhalves. MAME selects one half for characters and continues into the other half\nfor sprites, then uses the Pac-Man-bootleg graphics decode rather than the base\nGalaxian table. The board also has two sprite generators.\n\n- SURVIVING HARDWARE AND ART -\n\nZig Zag appears to have circulated as a pirate conversion kit rather than a\nstandardised dedicated cabinet. That is why no verified factory flyer or\ncabinet design is known. Surviving cabinets should be described as conversions,\nnot as proof of an original production cabinet.\n\nA surviving instruction card is preserved in the official MAME artwork\narchive. The archive credits TheGuru for scanning the card and Mr. Do for\ncleaning it and producing the MAME layout.\n\nA documented PCB repair found a board whose video side largely follows\nGalaxian while the other half is substantially redesigned. The repairer found\nno complete schematic set, which helps explain why preservation depends on\nMAME source, surviving boards, photographs and community catalogs.\n\n- SOURCES -\n\nMAME 0.288 source: src/mame/galaxian/galaxian.cpp, galaxian.h and galaxian_v.cpp\nLaunchBox Games Database: https://gamesdb.launchbox-app.com/games/details/133522-zig-zag\nMuseum of the Game, Dig Dug: https://www.arcade-museum.com/Videogame/dig-dug\nJalmis Zig Zag PCB repair log: https://wp.jalmis.se/category/arcade-games/zig-zag/\nprogetto-SNAPS cabinet and artwork catalogs: https://www.progettosnaps.net/\nMAME artwork archive (TheGuru / Mr. Do): https://archive.org/download/artwork_202505/zigzagb.zip\nGaming History: https://www.arcade-history.com/?id=3252",
  "historyCredit": "Curated from the preservation sources cited in the story"
